G1024.d:
βραχύς, -εῖα, -ύ, 
 [in LXX chiefly for מְעַט ;] 
short; 
  (a) of time; short, little: βραχύ, Acts.5:34; μετὰ β., Luke.22:58; β. τι, a short time, Heb.2:7, 9 RV, mg.; 
  (b) of distance: Acts.27:28; 
  (with) of quantity or value, little, few: John.6:7, Heb.2:7, 9 RV, txt.; pl., διὰ βραχέων, in few words, Heb.13:22.
† (AS)

G1074.d:
γενεά, -ᾶς, ἡ 
 (γίγνομαι), [in LXX chiefly for דּוֹר, דֹּר (Cremer, 148) ;] 
  1. race, stock, family (in NT, γέννημα, which see). 
  2. generation; 
  (a) of the contemporary members of a family: pl., Matt.1:17 (cf. Gen.31:3, מוֹלֶדֶת); metaphorically, of those alike in character, in bad sense, Matt.17:17, Mark.9:19, Luke.9:41 16:8, Acts.2:40; 
  (b) of all the people of a given period: Matt.24:34, Mark.13:30, Luke.21:32, Phil.2:15; pl., Luke.1:48; esp. of the Jewish people, Matt.11:16 12:39, 41 42, 45 16:4 23:36, Mark.8:12, 38 Luke.7:31 11:29, 30-32, 50, 51 17:25, Acts.13:36, Heb.3:10" (LXX) ; τὴν γ. αὐτοῦ τίς διηγήσεται, Acts.8:33 (LXX); 
  (with) the period covered by the life-time of a generation, used loosely in pl. of successive ages: Acts.14:16 15:21, Eph.3:5, Col.1:26; εἰς γενεὰς καὶ γ. (= לִדוֹר וָדוֹר, al.), Luke.34:17, al), Luke.1:50; εἰς πάσας τὰς γ. τοῦ αἰῶνος τῶν αἰώνων, Eph.3:21 (Ellic, in l.; DCG, i, 639f.).
 † (AS)

G1093.d:
γῆ, γῆς, ἡ, 
 [in LXX for אֲדָמָה ,אֶרֶץ, etc. ;] 
  1. the earth, world: Luke.21:35, Acts.1:8, Heb.11:13, Rev.3:10, al.; opposite to οὐρανός, Matt.5:18 11:25, Mark.13:27, al. 
  2. land; 
  (a) opposite to sea or water: Mark.4:1, Luke.5:3, John.6:21, al.; 
  (b) as subject to cultivation: Matt.13:5, Mark.4:8, Luke.13:7, Heb.6:7, al.; 
  (with) the ground: Matt.10:29, Mark.8:6, Luke.24:5, John.8:6, al.; 
  (d) a region, country: Luke.4:25, Rom.9:28, Jas.5:17; γῆ Ἰσραήλ, Matt.2:20, 21; Χαλδαίων, Acts.7:4; ἡ Ἰουδαία γῆ, John.3:22; with genitive of person(s), Acts.7:3.
 (AS)

G1106.d:
γνώμη, -ης, ἡ 
 (γιγνώσκω), [in LXX: Da TH 2:15 (דָּת), Ps 82(83):3 (סוֹד), elsewhere, chiefly II Es, for טְעֵם ;] 
  1. a means of knowing, a token (Theogn.). 
  2. In Attic writers, the mind, its operations and results (see Edwards, Lex., App., A); 
  (a) mind, understanding; 
  (b) purpose, intention: Acts.20:3; 
  (with) judgment, opinion: 1 Cor.1:10, Rev.17:13; 
  (d) counsel, advice: 1 Cor.7:25, 40 2 Cor.8:10, Phm 14; 
  (e) royal purpose, decree (as in Da, I and II Es): Rev.17:17 (Cremer, 671).
 † (AS)

G1121.d:
γράμμα, -τος, τό 
 (γράφω), [in LXX for סֵפֶר, etc. ;] 
  1. that which is traced or drawn, a picture. 
  2. that which is written; 
  (1) a character, letter: Gal.6:11; 
  (2) a writing, a written document; 
  (a) a bill or account: Luke.16:6, 7; 
  (b) a letter: Acts.28:21; 
  (with) τὰ ἱερὰ γ., the sacred writings, i.e. the OT: 2 Tim.3:15 (so in Philo, Vit. Mos., iii, 39) ; 
  (d) τὸ γ., the letter, the written word as an external authority in contrast with the direct influence of the Spirit as manifested in the new Covenant: Rom.2:27, 29 7:6, 2 Cor.3:6, 7; 
  (3) τὰ γ., letters, i.e. learning: John.7:15, Acts.26:24. (In π. an illiterate person is very frequently referred to as γράμματα μ᾽̀ εἰδότης, and this "never means anything else than inability to write": MM, Exp., x; but see also Cremer, 166; DCG, i, 202; ii, 584.)
 † (AS)

G1125.d:
γράφω, [in LXX chiefly for כּתב ]; 
  1. to scrape, graze (Hom.), and later (Hdt.) to sketch, draw. 
  2. to write; 
  (a) of forming or tracing letters on writing material: Jhn.8:[6], Gal.6:11, 2 Thess.3:17; 
  (b) to express in writing, commit to writing, record: Luke.1:63, John.19:21, 22 Rev.1:11, 19 al.; of scripture as a standing authority (Deiss., BS, 112ff.), γέγραπται, it  stands written (Luther), Matt.4:4, Mark.7:6, Luke.4:8, Rom.1:17, 1 Cor.1:31, al.; id. before ἐν, Mark.1:2, Acts.1:20, al.; with accusative, to write of: John.1:46, Rom.10:5; before περί, Matt.26:24, Mark.14:21, John.5:46; al.; with dative (WM, §31, 4), Luke.18:31; id. before ἵνα (M, Pr., 207f.), Mark.12:19, Luke.20:28; κατὰ τ. γεγραμμένον, 2 Cor.4:13; γεγραμμένον ἐστί, John.2:17; ἐγράφη, Rom.4:24; ἐπ᾽ αὐτῷ γεγραμμένα, John.12:16; 
  (with) of writing directions or information, with dative of person(s): Rom.15:15, 2 Cor.7:12, al.; 
  (d) of that which contains the record or message: βιβλίον, Mark.10:4, John.21:25, Rev.5:1; τίτλον, John.19:19; ἐπιστολήν, Acts.23:25; ἐντολήν, Mark.10:5 (cf. ἀπο-, ἐγ-, ἐπι-, κατα-, προ-).
 (AS)

G1140.d:
δαιμόνιον, -ου, τό
 (neut. of δαιμόνιος, -α, -ον, divine), [in LXX (so also in π; see MM, Exp., x) for שֵׁד, אֱלִיל, (frequently in To) ;] 
  1. as in cl.; 
  (a) the Divine power, Deity (Hdt., Plat., al.); 
  (b) an inferior divinity, deity or demon (as in magical π, Deiss., BS, 281; MM, Exp., x): ξένα δ., Acts.17:18. 
  2. (a) in OT, heathen deities, false gods (e.g. Deu.32:17, Psa.95 (96):5); 
  (b) in NT, evil spirits, demons: θύουσιν δαιμονίοις, 1 Cor.10:20; διδασκαλίαι δαιμονίων, 1 Tim.4:1; προσκυνεῖν τὰ δ., Rev.9:20; ἄρχων τῶν δ., Matt.9:34; especially (syn. with πνεῦμα ἀκάθαρτον) as operating upon and "possessing" (of. δαιμονίζομαι) men: Matt.11:18, and al.; εἰσέρχεσθαι δ. εἰς. . ., Luke.8:30; δ. ἔχειν, Luke.4:33; δ. ἐκβάλλειν, Matt.7:22 In the phrase πνεῦμα δαιμονίου ἀκαθάρτου, Luke.4:33, the wider cl. usage (1. b) is recognised, ἀκ. being elsewhere in NT, the epithet of πν., and δ. = πν. ἀκ. (see ICC, in l; Cremer, 168) 
 (AS)

G1166.d:
δείκνυμι, δεικνύω (see Bl., § 23, 1; Veitch, see word), 
 [in LXX chiefly for רָאָה hi. ;] 
to show, with accusative of thing(s) (of person(s)), dative of person(s); 
  (a) to show, exhibit: Matt.4:8 8:4, Mark.1:44 14:15, Luke.4:5 5:14 20:24 22:12 24:40, John.2:18 5:20 10:32; 20:20 Acts.7:3" (LXX), 1 Tim.6:15, Rev.17:1 21:9, 10 22:1, 8, pass., Heb.8:5 (LXX); 
  (b) to make known: Matt.16:21, John.14:8, 9, Acts.10:28, 1 Cor.12:31, Rev.1:1 4:1 22:6; 
  (with) to prove: Jas.2:18 3:13.†
 (AS)

G1175.d:
δεισιδαιμονία, -ας, ἡ 
 (δεισιδαίμων, which see); 
  1. fear of the gods; 
  (a) piety, religion; 
  (b) superstition. 
  2. Objectively, a religion: Acts.25:19 (Cremer, 72, 682).†
 (AS)

G1189.d:
δέομαι 
 (mid. of δέω, II, which see, as depon.) [in LXX for חנן, etc. ;] 
to want for oneself; 
  1. to want, need: 
  (a) absol.; 
  (b) with genitive 
  2. to beg, request, beseech, pray; 
  (i) in general: absol., Acts.26:3, WH; with genitive of person(s), Luke.5:12; 8:28; 38 (ἐδεῖτο; T, ἐδέετο, cf. Veitch, see word δέω) 9:38, Acts.8:34; 21:39, 2 Cor.5:20, Gal.4:12; before ἵνα, Luke.9:40; before τό, with inf., 2 Cor.10:2; with genitive of person(s) and of thing(s), 2 Cor.8:4; 
  (ii) of prayer to God: absol., Acts.4:31; before εἴ πως, Rom.1:10; ἵνα, Luke.21:36; 22:32; εἰς τό, 1 Thess.3:10; ὑπὲρ ἐμοῦ πρὸς τ. κύριον, ὅπως, Acts.8:24; with genitive, τοῦ κυρίου, ὅπως, Matt.9:38, Luke.10:2; τοῦ Θεοῦ, Acts.10:2; before εἰ ἄρα, Acts.8:22.† 
 SYN.: αἰτέω, and cf. δέησις. (AS)

G1219.d:
δημόσιος, -α, -ον
 (δῆμος), [in LXX: 2Ma.6:10, 3Ma.2:27 4:7 * ;] 
belonging to the people, public: Acts.5:18; dative fem. used adverbially (cl.); 
  (a) at the public expense, by public consent; 
  (b) publicly: Acts.16:37 18:28 20:20.†
 (AS)

G1223.d:
διά (before a vowel δ᾽, exc. Rom.8:10, 2 Cor.5:7, and in (Proper name)s; Tdf., Pr., 94), 
 prep. with genitive, accusative, as in cl.;  
  1. with genitive, through; 
  (i) of Place, after verbs of motion or action: Matt.2:12  12:43, Mark.2:23, Luke.4:30, John.4:4, 2 Cor.11:33, al.; σώζεσθαι (διας-) δ. πυρός, ὕδατος, 1 Cor.3:15, 1 Pet.3:20; βλέπειν δ. ἐσόπτρου, 1 Cor.13:12; metaphorically, of a state or condition: Rom.14:20, 2 Cor.2:4  5:7, 10; δ. γράμματος, ἀκροβυστίας (Lft., Notes, 263, 279), Rom.2:27  4:11; δι ̓ὑπομενῆς, Rom.8:25. 
  (ii) Of Time; 
  (a) during which: Matt.26:61, Mark.14:58, Luke.5:5; δ. παντὸς τοῦ ζῆν, Heb.2:15; δ. παντός (διαπαντός in Mark.5:5, Luke.24:53), always, continually, Matt.18:10, Acts.2:25" (LXX)  10:2 24:16, Rom.11:10" (LXX), 2 Thess.3:16, Heb.9:6  13:15. 
  (b) within which: Acts.1:3; δ. νυκτός, Acts.5:19  16:9 17:10 23:31; 
  (with) after which (Field, Notes, 20; Abbott, JG, 255f.): Mark.2:1, Acts.24:17, Gal.2:1. 
  (iii) Of the Means or Instrument; 
  (1) of the efficient cause (regarded also as the instrument): of God, Rom.11:36, 1 Cor.1:9, Gal.4:7, Heb.2:10  7:21; of Christ, Rom.1:8  5:1,  17, 1 Cor.15:21, 1 Pet.4:11, al.; δ. τ. ὑμῶν δεήσεως, Rom.1:12, 2 Cor.1:4, Gal.4:23, al.; 
  (2) of the agent, instrument or means; 
  (a) with genitive of person(s), Matt.11:2, Luke.1:70, John.1:17, Acts.1:16, Rom.2:16, 1 Cor.1:21, Eph.1:5, Heb.2:14, Rev.1:1, al.; ὑπὸ τ. κυρίου δ. τ. προφήτου (δ. τ. κυρίου, 1 Thess.4:2 (M, Th., in l.); Lft., Rev., 121f.), Matt.1:22  2:15, Rom.1:2; δ. ἐπιστολῆς ὡς δ. ἡμῶν (Field, Notes, 202), 2 Thess.2:2; δ. Σ. (NTD, 22), 1 Pet.5:12; 
  (b)  with genitive of thing(s) (where often the simple dative is used in cl.; Jannaris, Gr., 375), John.11:4, Acts.5:12; δ. τ. πίστεως, Rom.3:30; δ. λόγου θεοῦ, 1 Pet.1:23; δ. παραβολῆς, Luke.8:4; δουλεύειν δ. τ. ἀγάπης, Gal.5:13; δ. ἐπαγγελίας, Gal.3:18, 
  2. C. accusative; 
  (i) rarely, as with genitive, through (Hom), δ. μέσον Σαμαρίας (ICC, in l.; Bl., §42, 1; Robertson, Gr., 581), Luke.17:11. 
  (ii) by reason of, because of, for the sake of; 
  (a) with accusative of person(s) (M, Pr., 105), Mark.2:27, John.6:57 11:42, Rom.8:20; 
  (b) with accusative of thing(s), δ. φθόνον, Matt.27:18, Mark.15:10; δ. φόβον, John.7:13 20:19; δ. ἀγάπην, Eph.2:4; δ. τοῦτο, freq., for this cause, therefore, Matt.6:25, Mark.6:14, Luke.11:49, John.6:65, al.; id. before ὅτι, John.5:16 10:17, al.; δ. τί, why, Matt.9:11, 14 Mark.2:18, John.7:45, al.; δ. τό, with inf., Mark.5:4, Luke.9:7, Jas.4:2. 
  3. In composition, 
  (1) through, as in διαβαίνω; 
  (2) of separation, asunder, as in διασπάω; 
  (3) of distribution, abroad, as in διαγγέλλω; 
  (4) of transition, as διαλλάσσω; 
  (5) of "perfective" action (M, Pr., 112f., 115f.), as διαφύγω, διακαθαρίζω. 
 (AS)

G1228.d:
διάβολος, -ον
 (διαβάλλω, which see) [in LXX for שָׂטָן (as Job.1:6), exc. Est.7:4 8:1 (צַר, צָרַר) ;] 
slanderous, accusing falsely. As subst., ὁ, ἡ, δ.; 
  (a) generally (of. Es, ll. with), a slanderer, false accuser: 1 Tim.3:6, 7 (CGT, in l., but see infr.) 3:11, 2 Tim.3:3, Titus.2:3; 
  (b) as chiefly in LXX, of Satan, the Accuser, the Devil: Matt.4:1, 5, 8, 11 13:39 25:41, Luke.4:2, 3, 6, 13 8:12, John.13:2, Acts.10:38, Eph.4:27 6:11, 1 Tim.3:6, 7 (but see supr.), 2 Tim.2:26, Heb.2:14, Jas.4:7, 1 Pet.5:8, Ju 9, Rev.2:10 12:9, 12 20:2, 10; εἶναι ἐκ τοῦ δ., John.8:44, 1 John.3:8; τέκνα τοῦ δ., 1 John.3:10; υἱὸς, Acts.13:10; metaphorically, of Judas, John.6:70 (Cremer, 121; DCG, ii, 605).†
 (AS)

G1275.d:
δια-παντός, see διά, with genitive
  διά (before a vowel δ᾽, exc. Rom.8:10, 2 Cor.5:7, and in (Proper name)s; Tdf., Pr., 94), 
 prep. with genitive, accusative, as in cl.;  
  1. with genitive, through; 
  (i) of Place, after verbs of motion or action: Matt.2:12  12:43, Mark.2:23, Luke.4:30, John.4:4, 2 Cor.11:33, al.; σώζεσθαι (διας-) δ. πυρός, ὕδατος, 1 Cor.3:15, 1 Pet.3:20; βλέπειν δ. ἐσόπτρου, 1 Cor.13:12; metaphorically, of a state or condition: Rom.14:20, 2 Cor.2:4  5:7, 10; δ. γράμματος, ἀκροβυστίας (Lft., Notes, 263, 279), Rom.2:27  4:11; δι ̓ὑπομενῆς, Rom.8:25. 
  (ii) Of Time; 
  (a) during which: Matt.26:61, Mark.14:58, Luke.5:5; δ. παντὸς τοῦ ζῆν, Heb.2:15; δ. παντός (διαπαντός in Mark.5:5, Luke.24:53), always, continually, Matt.18:10, Acts.2:25" (LXX)  10:2 24:16, Rom.11:10" (LXX), 2 Thess.3:16, Heb.9:6  13:15. 
  (b) within which: Acts.1:3; δ. νυκτός, Acts.5:19  16:9 17:10 23:31; 
  (with) after which (Field, Notes, 20; Abbott, JG, 255f.): Mark.2:1, Acts.24:17, Gal.2:1. 
  (iii) Of the Means or Instrument; 
  (1) of the efficient cause (regarded also as the instrument): of God, Rom.11:36, 1 Cor.1:9, Gal.4:7, Heb.2:10  7:21; of Christ, Rom.1:8  5:1,  17, 1 Cor.15:21, 1 Pet.4:11, al.; δ. τ. ὑμῶν δεήσεως, Rom.1:12, 2 Cor.1:4, Gal.4:23, al.; 
  (2) of the agent, instrument or means; 
  (a) with genitive of person(s), Matt.11:2, Luke.1:70, John.1:17, Acts.1:16, Rom.2:16, 1 Cor.1:21, Eph.1:5, Heb.2:14, Rev.1:1, al.; ὑπὸ τ. κυρίου δ. τ. προφήτου (δ. τ. κυρίου, 1 Thess.4:2 (M, Th., in l.); Lft., Rev., 121f.), Matt.1:22  2:15, Rom.1:2; δ. ἐπιστολῆς ὡς δ. ἡμῶν (Field, Notes, 202), 2 Thess.2:2; δ. Σ. (NTD, 22), 1 Pet.5:12; 
  (b)  with genitive of thing(s) (where often the simple dative is used in cl.; Jannaris, Gr., 375), John.11:4, Acts.5:12; δ. τ. πίστεως, Rom.3:30; δ. λόγου θεοῦ, 1 Pet.1:23; δ. παραβολῆς, Luke.8:4; δουλεύειν δ. τ. ἀγάπης, Gal.5:13; δ. ἐπαγγελίας, Gal.3:18, 
  2. C. accusative; 
  (i) rarely, as with genitive, through (Hom), δ. μέσον Σαμαρίας (ICC, in l.; Bl., §42, 1; Robertson, Gr., 581), Luke.17:11. 
  (ii) by reason of, because of, for the sake of; 
  (a) with accusative of person(s) (M, Pr., 105), Mark.2:27, John.6:57 11:42, Rom.8:20; 
  (b) with accusative of thing(s), δ. φθόνον, Matt.27:18, Mark.15:10; δ. φόβον, John.7:13 20:19; δ. ἀγάπην, Eph.2:4; δ. τοῦτο, freq., for this cause, therefore, Matt.6:25, Mark.6:14, Luke.11:49, John.6:65, al.; id. before ὅτι, John.5:16 10:17, al.; δ. τί, why, Matt.9:11, 14 Mark.2:18, John.7:45, al.; δ. τό, with inf., Mark.5:4, Luke.9:7, Jas.4:2. 
  3. In composition, 
  (1) through, as in διαβαίνω; 
  (2) of separation, asunder, as in διασπάω; 
  (3) of distribution, abroad, as in διαγγέλλω; 
  (4) of transition, as διαλλάσσω; 
  (5) of "perfective" action (M, Pr., 112f., 115f.), as διαφύγω, διακαθαρίζω. 
 (AS)

G1296.d:
δια-ταγή, -ῆς, ἡ
 (διατάσσω), [in LXX: Est.4:11 (פַּרְשֶׁגֶן)* ;] 
in late writers (Deiss., LAE, 86 ff.) for cl. διάταξις (WH., however, is found in LXX, Ps.119:91, al.); 
  (a) disposition (cf. διάταξις for צָבָא; Sm., 4Ki.23:4, Jer.8:2 19:13); εἰς διαταγὰς ἀγγέλων, AV, by the disposition of angels (Alf., in l, Field, Notes, 116; but see infr.): Acts.7:53; 
  (b) ordinance (C. I. 3465): Rom.13:2, Acts.7:53, R, txt. (and see mg.; Page and EGT, in l; but also see supr.).†
 (AS)

G1329.d:
δι-ερμηνεύω 
 [in LXX: 2Ma.1:36 * ;] 
intensive of ἐρμηνεύω, to interpret; 
  (a) to explain, expound: with accusative, Luke.24:27; absol., 1 Cor.12:30 14:5 14:13 14:27; 
  (b) to translate: Acts.9:36.†
 (AS)

G1344.d:
δικαιόω, -ῶ
 (δίκαιος), [in LXX chiefly for צָדַק pi., hi., (1) as Eze.16:51, Jer.3:11 (cf. NT usage); (2) as Deu.25:1; Exod.23:7, Isa.50:8 ;] 
  1. in cl., 
  (a) with accusative of thing(s), to deem right; 
  (b) with accusative of person(s), to do one justice; pass., δικαιοῦσθαι, to be treated rightly, opposite to ἀδικεῖσθαι. 
  2. In NT, as in LXX, and as usual with verbs in -όω from adjectives of moral meaning; 
  (1) to show to be righteous: Matt.11:19, Luke.7:35, Rom.3:4" (LXX), 1 Tim.3:16; 
  (2) to declare, pronounce righteous: Luke.7:29 10:29 16:15 18:14, Rom.2:13 3:24, 26 3:28 4:5 8:30, 33, Titus.3:7; before ἀπό, Matt.11:19, Luke.7:35, Acts.13:39, Rom.6:7; ἐκ πίστεως, Rom.3:30 5:1, Gal.2:16 3:8, 24; ἐξ ἔργων, Rom.3:20 (LXX) Rom.4:2, Gal.2:16, Jas.2:21, 24-25; ἐκ τ. λόγων, Matt.12:37; διὰ τ. πίστεως, Rom.3:30; with dative, Rom.3:24, 28, Titus.3:7; before ἐν, Acts.13:39, Rom.3:4 5:9, 1 Cor.4:4 6:11, Gal.2:17 3:11 5:4, 1 Tim.3:16 (see Cremer, 193, 693; DB, ii, 826 ff.).†
 (AS)

G1411.d:
δύναμις, -εως, ἡ 
 (δύναμαι), [in LXX for עֹז ,גּבוּרָה ,צָבָה ,(חֵיל) חַיִל, etc.; 35 words in all ;] 
power, might, strength; relatively, ability, power to perform: Matt.25:15, Acts.3:12, Heb.11:11; κατὰ δ., 2 Cor.8:3; παρὰ δ., ib.; ὑπὲρ δ., 2 Cor.1:8; of pecuniary ability, 2 Cor.8:3, Rev.18:3; absol., power, might: Luke.24:49, Acts.1:8; opposite to ἀσθένεια, 1 Cor.15:43; ἡ δ. τ. ἁμαρτίας, 1 Cor.15:56; of power in action, Rom.1:16, 20 1 Cor.1:18, Phil.3:10, al.; ἡ δ. τ. θεοῦ, Matt.22:29, Mark.12:24, Rom.1:20, al.; opposite to μόρφωσις, 2 Tim.3:5; in doxologies, Rev.4:11 7:12, al.; ἐν δ., Mark.9:1, Luke.4:36, Rom.1:4, al.; of the power of performing miracles, Acts.6:8, 2 Thess.2:9; pl., Matt.13:54, Mark.6:14, Gal.3:5, al.; of the force or meaning of a word (Plat., al.), 1 Cor.14:11. By meton., of persons or things; 
  (a) of God, Matt.26:64, Mark.14:62 (Dalman, Words, 200ff.); 
  (b) of angels, Rom.8:38, Eph.1:21, 1 Pet.3:22; 
  (with) of armies, pl. [LXX for צְבָאוֹת ], metaphorically, of the stars, Matt.24:29, Mark.13:25, Luke.21:26; 
  (d) of that wh. manifests God's power: Christ, 1 Cor.1:24; τ. εὐαγγέλλιον, Rom.1:16; ἡ δ. τ. κυρίου, 1 Cor.5:4; 
  (e) of mighty works (Tr., Syn., §xci), δ. ποιεῖν, Mark.6:5 9:39; pl., Matt.7:22, Mark.6:2, Luke.10:13, al.; σημεῖακ. δ., Acts.8:13; δ. κ. τέρατα κ. σημεῖα, Acts.2:22, 2 Cor.12:12. 
 SYN.: βία, ἐνέργεια, ἐξουσία, ἰσχύς, κράτος (see Tr., l.with; Cremer, 218, 236; DB, i, 616; iv, 29; DCG, i, 607; ii, 188). (AS)

G1437.d:
ἐάν, contr. fr. εἰ ἄν, 
 conditional particle, 
representing something as "under certain circumstances actual or liable to happen," but not so definitely expected as in the case of εἰ with ind. (Bl., §65, 4; cf. John.13:17, 1 Cor.7:36), if haply, if; 
  1. with subjc. (cl.); 
  (a) pres.: Matt.6:22, Luke.10:6, John.7:17, Rom.2:25, 26 al.; 
{  (b) aor. (= Lat. fut. pf.): Matt.4:9 16:26 (cf. ptcp. in Luke.9:25; M, Pr., 230), Mark.3:24, Luke.14:34, John.5:43, Rom.7:2, al.; = cl. εἰ, with opt., John.9:22 11:57, Acts.9:2; as Heb. אִם = ὅταν, John.12:32 14:3, I John.2:28 3:2, Heb.3:7" (LXX) . 
  2. C. indic, (as in late writers, fr. Arist. on; see WH, App., 171; VD, MGr. 2, App., §77; Deiss., BS, 201f., LAE, 155, 254; M, Pr., 168, 187; Bl., §65, 4); 
  (a) fut.: Matt.18:19 T, Luke.19:40,  Acts.7:7; 
  (b) pres.: 1 Thess.3:8 (see Milligan, in l.). 
  3. With other particles: ἐ. καί (Bl., §65, 6), Gal.6:1; ἐ. μή (M, Pr., 185, 187; Bl., l.with), with subjc. pres., Matt.10:13, 1 Cor.8:8, Jas.2:17, 1 John.3:21; aor., Matt.6:15, Mark.3:27, John.3:3, Rom.10:15, Gal.1:8 2:16 (see Lft., Ellic., in ll.); ἐ. τε . . . ἐ. τε, [in LXX for אִם . . . אִם, Est.19:13, al.,] Rom.14:8. 
  4. = cl. ἄν (which see) after relat. pronouns and adverbs (Tdf., Pr., 96; WH, App., 173; M, Pr., 42f.; Bl., §26, 4; Mayser, 152f.; Deiss., BS, 202ff.): ὃς ἐ., Matt.5:19, Mark.6:22, 23 Luke.17:32, 1 Cor.6:18, al.; ὅπου ἐ., Matt.8:19; ὁσάκις ἐ., Rev.11:6; οὗ ἐ., 1 Cor.16:6; καθὸ ἐ., 2 Cor.8:12; ὅστις ἐ., Gal.5:10. 
 (AS)

G1438.d:
ἑαυτοῦ, -ῆς, -οῦ, dative -ῷ, etc., accusative -όν, etc., pi. -ῶν, etc. (Att. contr. αὑτοῦ, etc); 
 reflex pron.; 
  1. prop, of 3rd person (Lat. sui, sibi, se), of himself, herself, itself, etc.: Matt.27:42, Mark.15:31, Luke.23:35, al.; added to a middle verb, διεμερίσαντο ἑαυτοῖς, John.19:24; to an active verb, Acts.14:14 (M, Pr., 157); ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ, Luke.12:57 21:30, John.5:19, al. (see: ἀπό); δι᾽ ἑαυτοῦ, Rom.14:14; ἐν ἑ., Matt.3:9, Mark.5:30, al.; εἰς ἑ., Luke.15:17; καθ᾽ ἑαυτόν, Acts.28:16, Jas.2:17; παρ᾽ ἑαυτῷ, at his own house, 1 Cor.16:2; πρὸς ἑ., with, to himself, Luke.18:11; as poss. pron. (with emphasis weakened; see M, Pr., 87f.), τ. ἑαυτῶν νεκρούς, Luke.9:60. 
  2. As reflexive 1st and 2nd of person(s) (so also frequently in cl., chiefly poetry), Matt.23:31, Mark.9:50, Rom.8:23, 1 Thess.2:8, al. 
  3. In pl., for reciprocal pron., ἀλλήλων, -οις, -ους, of one another, etc.: Matt.21:38, Mark.16:3, Eph.5:19, al. 
 (AS)
